# The people of the Mabinogi (November 2026): a page for each person of the tales, and for the people who kept them.
# Ifan asked for "a lot of information about the Mabinogi" (12 November 2026); the coordinator listed Pwyll, Rhiannon,
# Pryderi, Branwen, Manawydan, Math, Gwydion, Blodeuwedd, Lleu and Arianrhod.
# People of the tales are told in the words of the tales and the Triads ("the tale tells"), with respect.
# Their date is "The Four Branches" (date_cy in build.py, sorting in data.py). Same entry format as people2a.py;
# people2.py gathers this list with the others. GROUPS sets out the "People of the Mabinogi" block on each of their pages.
# Real people (the patrons, the scribe, Lady Charlotte Guest's helpers) are from the Dictionary of Welsh Biography.
from people2a import P

D4 = "The Four Branches"

PEOPLE = [
 # ---------- the First Branch ----------
 P("pwyll", "ancient", D4, "Pwyll", "Pwyll", "Prince of Dyfed and Head of Annwn", "Pendefig Dyfed a Phen Annwn", "Pwyll", "rulers",
   "Prince of the seven cantrefs of Dyfed in the First Branch of the Mabinogi, who ruled the Otherworld for a year in the shape of its king, and won Rhiannon.",
   "Tywysog saith cantref Dyfed yng Nghainc Gyntaf y Mabinogi, a lywodraethodd Annwn am flwyddyn ym mhryd ei brenin, ac a enillodd Riannon.",
   ["Pwyll Pendefig Dyfed, Pwyll Prince of Dyfed, is the hero of the First Branch of the Mabinogi. His court was at Arberth, Narberth in Pembrokeshire, and he was lord of the seven cantrefs of Dyfed. His name means ‘wisdom’ or ‘good sense’, and the tale shows him learning it.",
    "Out hunting in Glyn Cuch, he wronged Arawn, king of Annwn, the Otherworld, by driving off his hounds. To make it right, Pwyll took Arawn’s shape and ruled Annwn for a year and a day, and he kept faith with his friend so truly that he never once touched Arawn’s queen, though she lay beside him every night. He overcame Arawn’s enemy Hafgan with a single blow at the ford, and from then on he was called Pen Annwn, Head of the Otherworld.",
    "On the mound of Gorsedd Arberth he saw Rhiannon ride by on her white horse, and after three days of chasing her he learned the lesson of courtesy: he only had to ask her to wait. He won her from her rival Gwawl with the trick of the bag. When their son vanished on the night he was born and Rhiannon was wrongly blamed, Pwyll would not put her away. When the boy came home from Gwent, Pwyll gave him to Pendaran Dyfed to foster, and the boy was named Pryderi.",
    "Pwyll is remembered as a lord who learned from his mistakes and kept his word, and the friendship between Pwyll and Arawn, with gifts of horses, hounds and hawks for the rest of their lives, is one of the warmest things in the Four Branches."],
   ["Pwyll Pendefig Dyfed yw arwr Cainc Gyntaf y Mabinogi. Roedd ei lys yn Arberth yn Sir Benfro, ac ef oedd arglwydd saith cantref Dyfed. Ystyr ei enw yw ‘doethineb’ neu ‘synnwyr’, ac mae’r chwedl yn dangos sut y dysgodd ef.",
    "Wrth hela yng Nglyn Cuch, gwnaeth gam ag Arawn, brenin Annwn, drwy yrru ei gŵn ymaith. I wneud iawn, cymerodd Pwyll bryd Arawn a llywodraethu Annwn am flwyddyn a diwrnod, a chadwodd ffydd â’i gyfaill mor gywir fel na chyffyrddodd erioed â brenhines Arawn, er iddi orwedd wrth ei ochr bob nos. Trechodd elyn Arawn, Hafgan, ag un ergyd wrth y rhyd, ac o hynny ymlaen fe’i gelwid yn Ben Annwn.",
    "Ar dwmpath Gorsedd Arberth gwelodd Riannon yn marchogaeth heibio ar ei march gwyn, ac wedi tridiau o’i hymlid dysgodd wers cwrteisi: dim ond gofyn iddi aros oedd raid. Enillodd hi oddi wrth ei chystadleuydd Gwawl â chast y god. Pan ddiflannodd eu mab y noson y’i ganed a rhoi’r bai ar gam ar Riannon, ni fynnai Pwyll ei hysgaru. Pan ddaeth y bachgen adref o Went, rhoddodd Pwyll ef i Bendaran Dyfed i’w fagu, a rhoddwyd yr enw Pryderi arno.",
    "Fe’i cofir yn arglwydd a ddysgodd o’i gamgymeriadau ac a gadwodd ei air, ac mae’r cyfeillgarwch rhwng Pwyll ac Arawn, â rhoddion o feirch, cŵn a hebogau weddill eu hoes, yn un o’r pethau cynhesaf yn y Pedair Cainc."]),
 P("rhiannon", "ancient", D4, "Rhiannon", "Rhiannon", "Queen of Dyfed, the lady of the white horse", "Brenhines Dyfed, arglwyddes y march gwyn", "Rhiannon", "rulers",
   "Daughter of Hefeydd Hen, wife of Pwyll and later of Manawydan, and mother of Pryderi. The wisest and most patient woman of the Four Branches, whose birds sang the dead awake.",
   "Merch Hefeydd Hen, gwraig Pwyll ac wedyn Manawydan, a mam Pryderi. Gwraig ddoethaf ac amyneddgaraf y Pedair Cainc, y deffrôi ei hadar y meirw.",
   ["Rhiannon, daughter of Hefeydd Hen, rides into the First Branch on a tall white horse, at a slow and steady pace that no rider can ever catch. She had chosen Pwyll, prince of Dyfed, for herself, and had come to find him rather than be given to a man she did not want. When Pwyll foolishly promised her away to her rival Gwawl, it was Rhiannon who made the plan of the magic bag that won her back.",
    "When their son vanished on the night of his birth, the women set to watch him smeared her with a puppy’s blood and swore that she had killed him. Rather than argue with them, Rhiannon took her penance: for seven years she sat by the horse block at the gate of Arberth, told her story to every stranger, and offered to carry them on her back. When Teyrnon brought the boy home, her first words gave him his name: ‘My worry would be over, if that were true,’ and he was called Pryderi.",
    "In the Third Branch she married Manawydan, and was caught by enchantment with her son in a vanishing fort, and made to wear the collars of asses until Manawydan set her free. Her birds, the Adar Rhiannon, are famous in Welsh tradition: in the Second Branch they sang for seven years at Harlech, and in Culhwch and Olwen it is said that they ‘wake the dead and lull the living to sleep’.",
    "Many scholars hear in her name the old British Rigantona, ‘great queen’, and see in her horse and her patience the memory of a great goddess of the Britons. In the tales she is simply the noblest of women: clever, generous, faithful and calm under the worst of wrongs. Fleetwood Mac’s song Rhiannon, written by Stevie Nicks, took her name around the world."],
   ["Daw Rhiannon ferch Hefeydd Hen i’r Gainc Gyntaf ar farch gwyn tal, ar gam araf a chyson na all yr un marchog byth ei ddal. Roedd hi wedi dewis Pwyll, tywysog Dyfed, iddi hi ei hun, ac wedi dod i chwilio amdano yn hytrach na chael ei rhoi i ŵr nad oedd arni ei eisiau. Pan addawodd Pwyll hi’n ffôl i’w chystadleuydd Gwawl, Rhiannon a wnaeth y cynllun â’r god hud a’i henillodd yn ôl.",
    "Pan ddiflannodd eu mab y noson y’i ganed, irodd y gwragedd a osodwyd i’w wylio hi â gwaed ci bach a thyngu iddi ei ladd. Yn hytrach na dadlau â hwy, cymerodd Rhiannon ei phenyd: am saith mlynedd eisteddodd wrth yr esgynfaen ger porth Arberth, adrodd ei hanes wrth bob dieithryn, a chynnig eu cario ar ei chefn. Pan ddaeth Teyrnon â’r bachgen adref, ei geiriau cyntaf a roddodd ei enw iddo: ‘Oedd esgor fy mhryder i, pe gwir hynny,’ a galwyd ef yn Bryderi.",
    "Yn y Drydedd Gainc priododd Fanawydan, a’i dal gan hud gyda’i mab mewn caer a ddiflannodd, a’i gorfodi i wisgo mwnwgl asynnod nes i Fanawydan ei rhyddhau. Mae ei hadar, Adar Rhiannon, yn enwog yn nhraddodiad Cymru: yn yr Ail Gainc canasant am saith mlynedd yn Harlech, ac yn Culhwch ac Olwen dywedir eu bod yn ‘deffro’r meirw ac yn suo’r byw i gysgu’.",
    "Mae llawer o ysgolheigion yn clywed yn ei henw yr hen enw Brythonig Rigantona, ‘brenhines fawr’, ac yn gweld yn ei march a’i hamynedd gof am dduwies fawr y Brythoniaid. Yn y chwedlau hi yw’r fonheddicaf o wragedd: yn glyfar, yn hael, yn ffyddlon ac yn dawel dan y camweddau gwaethaf. Aeth cân Fleetwood Mac, Rhiannon, a ysgrifennwyd gan Stevie Nicks, â’i henw o gwmpas y byd."]),
 P("pryderi", "ancient", D4, "Pryderi", "Pryderi", "Prince of Dyfed, the one hero of all four Branches", "Tywysog Dyfed, unig arwr y pedair Cainc", "Pryderi", "rulers",
   "Son of Pwyll and Rhiannon, lost on the night he was born and found in Gwent. He is the only hero who appears in all Four Branches of the Mabinogi: born in the First, he dies in the Fourth.",
   "Mab Pwyll a Rhiannon, a gollwyd y noson y’i ganed ac a gafwyd yng Ngwent. Ef yw’r unig arwr sy’n ymddangos ym mhob un o Bedair Cainc y Mabinogi: fe’i genir yn y Gyntaf, a bydd farw yn y Bedwaredd.",
   ["Pryderi son of Pwyll is the thread that runs through the whole of the Mabinogi. He vanished from his mother’s side on the night he was born at Arberth, and was found by Teyrnon, lord of Gwent Is Coed, lying by his stable door on May Eve, wrapped in brocaded silk. Teyrnon and his wife raised him as Gwri Wallt Euryn, Gwri of the Golden Hair, until Teyrnon saw the face of Pwyll in the boy and brought him home. Rhiannon’s words, ‘my worry is over’, gave him his true name, from pryder, ‘worry’.",
    "He grew up to rule Dyfed well, and won the three cantrefs of Ystrad Tywi and the four of Ceredigion, the seven cantrefs of Seisyllwg. He married Cigfa, daughter of Gwyn Gloyw. In the Second Branch he sailed with Bendigeidfran to Ireland and was one of the seven who came home. In the Third he gave his own mother and his lands to his friend Manawydan, and was caught by enchantment in a vanishing fort until Manawydan freed him.",
    "In the Fourth Branch Gwydion tricked him out of the swine that Arawn of Annwn had sent him, and Pryderi marched north to Gwynedd to take revenge. To spare his people, he asked to settle the war in single combat with Gwydion. He fell at Y Felenrhyd on the Dwyryd and was buried at Maentwrog: ‘and there is his grave’. He is remembered as a brave, generous and loyal lord who died for his people."],
   ["Pryderi fab Pwyll yw’r edau sy’n rhedeg drwy’r Mabinogi cyfan. Diflannodd oddi wrth ochr ei fam y noson y’i ganed yn Arberth, a chafwyd ef gan Teyrnon, arglwydd Gwent Is Coed, yn gorwedd wrth ddrws ei stabl Nos Galan Mai, wedi’i lapio mewn pali. Magodd Teyrnon a’i wraig ef fel Gwri Wallt Euryn, nes i Teyrnon weld wyneb Pwyll yn y bachgen a dod ag ef adref. Rhoddodd geiriau Rhiannon, ‘esgor fy mhryder’, ei enw cywir iddo.",
    "Tyfodd i lywodraethu Dyfed yn dda, ac enillodd dri chantref Ystrad Tywi a phedwar Ceredigion, saith cantref Seisyllwg. Priododd Gigfa, merch Gwyn Gloyw. Yn yr Ail Gainc hwyliodd gyda Bendigeidfran i Iwerddon ac roedd yn un o’r saith a ddaeth adref. Yn y Drydedd rhoddodd ei fam ei hun a’i diroedd i’w gyfaill Manawydan, a’i ddal gan hud mewn caer a ddiflannodd nes i Fanawydan ei ryddhau.",
    "Yn y Bedwaredd Gainc twyllodd Gwydion ef o’r moch a anfonasai Arawn o Annwn iddo, a gorymdeithiodd Pryderi i’r gogledd i Wynedd i ddial. I arbed ei bobl, gofynnodd am setlo’r rhyfel mewn ymladdfa un-ac-un â Gwydion. Syrthiodd yn y Felenrhyd ar afon Dwyryd a’i gladdu ym Maentwrog: ‘ac yno y mae ei fedd’. Fe’i cofir yn arglwydd dewr, hael a ffyddlon a fu farw dros ei bobl."]),
 P("arawn", "ancient", D4, "Arawn", "Arawn", "King of Annwn, the Otherworld", "Brenin Annwn", "Arawn", "rulers",
   "King of Annwn, the Otherworld, in the First Branch, with his white hounds with red ears. He changed places with Pwyll for a year and they became friends for life.",
   "Brenin Annwn yn y Gainc Gyntaf, â’i gŵn gwynion clustgoch. Cyfnewidiodd le â Phwyll am flwyddyn a buont yn gyfeillion am oes.",
   ["Arawn, king of Annwn, rides into the First Branch on a great grey horse, in grey hunting clothes, behind a pack of shining white hounds with red ears, the hounds of the Otherworld. When Pwyll drove them from their kill in Glyn Cuch, Arawn asked him to make amends by taking his shape and his kingdom for a year, and by fighting his rival Hafgan at the ford.",
    "When the year was over and Arawn learned that Pwyll had ruled his kingdom well, won it whole from Hafgan, and never touched his queen, he gave him his friendship for ever. They sent each other horses, hounds and hawks, and in the Fourth Branch it is Arawn’s gift of swine to Pryderi, Pwyll’s son, that Gwydion steals. Arawn is the courteous king of the Otherworld: a just ruler who rewards faithfulness."],
   ["Daw Arawn, brenin Annwn, i’r Gainc Gyntaf ar farch llwyd mawr, mewn dillad hela llwydion, y tu ôl i haid o gŵn claerwyn clustgoch, cŵn Annwn. Pan yrrodd Pwyll hwy oddi ar eu hysglyfaeth yng Nglyn Cuch, gofynnodd Arawn iddo wneud iawn drwy gymryd ei bryd a’i deyrnas am flwyddyn, ac ymladd â’i elyn Hafgan wrth y rhyd.",
    "Pan ddaeth y flwyddyn i ben a chlywed Arawn i Bwyll lywodraethu ei deyrnas yn dda, ei hennill yn gyfan oddi wrth Hafgan, a pheidio byth â chyffwrdd â’i frenhines, rhoddodd ei gyfeillgarwch iddo am byth. Anfonent feirch, cŵn a hebogau at ei gilydd, ac yn y Bedwaredd Gainc rhodd Arawn o foch i Bryderi, mab Pwyll, a ddwg Gwydion. Arawn yw brenin cwrtais Annwn: rheolwr cyfiawn sy’n gwobrwyo ffyddlondeb."]),
 P("teyrnon", "ancient", D4, "Teyrnon Twrf Liant", "Teyrnon Twrf Liant", "Lord of Gwent Is Coed, who found Pryderi", "Arglwydd Gwent Is Coed, a gafodd Bryderi", "", "rulers",
   "Lord of Gwent Is Coed in the First Branch, who saved a newborn boy from a monstrous claw on May Eve, raised him, and brought him home to Rhiannon.",
   "Arglwydd Gwent Is Coed yn y Gainc Gyntaf, a achubodd fachgen newydd-anedig rhag crafanc anferth Nos Galan Mai, a’i fagu, a dod ag ef adref at Riannon.",
   ["Teyrnon Twrf Liant was lord of Gwent Is Coed, the land between the Usk and the Wye, and the best man in the world, the tale says. Every May Eve his fine mare foaled, and every year the foal vanished. One year he kept watch, armed, and when a huge claw came through the window to seize the newborn colt he cut off the arm at the elbow. When he came back from chasing the noise into the dark, he found a baby boy lying by the door in a mantle of brocaded silk.",
    "He and his wife raised the boy as their own, as Gwri Wallt Euryn, until Teyrnon heard of Rhiannon’s penance at Arberth and saw that the boy had the face of Pwyll. He did what was right at once, though it cost him the boy he loved, and took him home. Offered great rewards, he took nothing. His name is from the old British Tigernonos, ‘great lord’, and he is the very picture of an honourable man."],
   ["Teyrnon Twrf Liant oedd arglwydd Gwent Is Coed, y tir rhwng Wysg a Gwy, a’r gŵr gorau yn y byd, medd y chwedl. Bob Nos Galan Mai byddai ei gaseg hardd yn bwrw ebol, a bob blwyddyn diflannai’r ebol. Un flwyddyn cadwodd wyliadwriaeth, yn arfog, a phan ddaeth crafanc enfawr drwy’r ffenestr i gipio’r ebol newydd ei eni torrodd y fraich wrth y penelin. Pan ddaeth yn ôl o ymlid y twrw i’r tywyllwch, cafodd fachgen bach yn gorwedd wrth y drws mewn llen o bali.",
    "Magodd ef a’i wraig y bachgen fel eu plentyn eu hunain, fel Gwri Wallt Euryn, nes i Teyrnon glywed am benyd Rhiannon yn Arberth a gweld bod gan y bachgen wyneb Pwyll. Gwnaeth yr hyn oedd yn iawn ar unwaith, er iddo gostio iddo’r bachgen a garai, a’i ddwyn adref. Pan gynigiwyd gwobrau mawr iddo, ni chymerodd ddim. Daw ei enw o’r hen Frythoneg Tigernonos, ‘arglwydd mawr’, ac ef yw’r union ddarlun o ŵr anrhydeddus."]),
 P("cigfa", "ancient", D4, "Cigfa", "Cigfa", "Wife of Pryderi", "Gwraig Pryderi", "", "rulers",
   "Daughter of Gwyn Gloyw and wife of Pryderi, one of the four left in the empty land of Dyfed in the Third Branch.",
   "Merch Gwyn Gloyw a gwraig Pryderi, un o’r pedwar a adawyd yng ngwlad wag Dyfed yn y Drydedd Gainc.",
   ["Cigfa, daughter of Gwyn Gloyw, married Pryderi at the end of the First Branch and became lady of Dyfed. In the Third Branch she was one of the four who sat on Gorsedd Arberth when the mist fell and the land was emptied. When Pryderi and Rhiannon vanished with the enchanted fort, she was left alone with Manawydan, and wept, fearing for herself. Manawydan swore to be a true friend to her, and kept his word.",
    "She was with him through the hard years that followed, in the shoemakers’ shops of England and in the empty fields of Dyfed, and it was Cigfa who questioned whether a lord should hang a mouse. Her patience was rewarded when Pryderi and Rhiannon were set free, and the land came back to life around them."],
   ["Priododd Cigfa ferch Gwyn Gloyw â Phryderi ar ddiwedd y Gainc Gyntaf a dod yn arglwyddes Dyfed. Yn y Drydedd Gainc roedd yn un o’r pedwar a eisteddai ar Orsedd Arberth pan ddisgynnodd y niwl a gwagio’r wlad. Pan ddiflannodd Pryderi a Rhiannon gyda’r gaer hud, fe’i gadawyd ar ei phen ei hun gyda Manawydan, ac wylodd, gan ofni drosti ei hun. Tyngodd Manawydan y byddai’n gyfaill cywir iddi, a chadwodd ei air.",
    "Bu gydag ef drwy’r blynyddoedd caled a ddilynodd, yng ngweithdai’r cryddion yn Lloegr ac ym meysydd gwag Dyfed, a Chigfa a holodd a ddylai arglwydd grogi llygoden. Gwobrwywyd ei hamynedd pan ryddhawyd Pryderi a Rhiannon, a phan ddaeth y wlad yn fyw eto o’u cwmpas."]),

 # ---------- the Second Branch ----------
 P("branwen", "ancient", D4, "Branwen ferch Llŷr", "Branwen ferch Llŷr", "Queen of Ireland, one of the Three Chief Maidens of Britain", "Brenhines Iwerddon, un o Dair Prif Rhiain Prydain", "Branwen", "rulers",
   "Sister of Bendigeidfran and Manawydan, given in marriage to the king of Ireland. Her starling carried her letter across the sea, and her grave is on the bank of the Alaw in Anglesey.",
   "Chwaer Bendigeidfran a Manawydan, a roddwyd yn wraig i frenin Iwerddon. Cludodd ei drudwen ei llythyr dros y môr, ac mae ei bedd ar lan Alaw ym Môn.",
   ["Branwen, daughter of Llŷr, is the heroine of the Second Branch of the Mabinogi. She was the sister of Bendigeidfran, Brân the Blessed, crowned king of the Island of the Mighty, and of Manawydan, and the tale calls her one of the Three Chief Maidens of this island, and the fairest maiden in the world. Her name is often read as ‘white raven’ or ‘fair breast’.",
    "She was given in marriage at Aberffraw to Matholwch, king of Ireland, to join the two islands in peace, and she sailed from Aber Menai as a queen. In Ireland she was honoured at first and bore a son, Gwern. But the men of Ireland had not forgotten the insult her half-brother Efnysien had done to their king’s horses, and they made her suffer for it. She was sent to work in the kitchen, and the butcher struck her every day.",
    "Branwen did not despair. She tamed a starling, taught it to speak and to know her brother, tied a letter under its wing, and sent it over the sea. It found Bendigeidfran at Caer Seint in Arfon, and the whole host of Britain came to save her. When the terrible war was over and only seven came home with her, she landed at Aber Alaw in Anglesey, looked back at Ireland and at Britain, and said: ‘Woe is me that I was ever born. Two good islands have been laid waste because of me.’ Her heart broke, and she was buried in a four-sided grave on the bank of the Alaw.",
    "Bedd Branwen, a Bronze Age cairn near Llanddeusant in Anglesey, has borne her name for centuries. Branwen stands for every woman who has paid the price of other people’s quarrels, and her starling is one of the best-loved images in Welsh literature."],
   ["Branwen ferch Llŷr yw arwres Ail Gainc y Mabinogi. Roedd yn chwaer i Fendigeidfran, brenin coronog Ynys y Cedyrn, ac i Fanawydan, ac mae’r chwedl yn ei galw’n un o Dair Prif Rhiain yr ynys hon, a’r forwyn decaf yn y byd. Darllenir ei henw’n aml fel ‘brân wen’ neu ‘bron wen’.",
    "Fe’i rhoddwyd yn wraig yn Aberffraw i Fatholwch, brenin Iwerddon, i uno’r ddwy ynys mewn hedd, a hwyliodd o Aber Menai yn frenhines. Yn Iwerddon cafodd barch ar y dechrau ac esgor ar fab, Gwern. Ond nid oedd gwŷr Iwerddon wedi anghofio’r sarhad a wnaethai ei hanner brawd Efnysien i feirch eu brenin, a gwnaethant iddi ddioddef amdano. Fe’i hanfonwyd i weithio yn y gegin, a thrawai’r cigydd hi bob dydd.",
    "Ni anobeithiodd Branwen. Dofodd ddrudwen, ei dysgu i siarad ac i adnabod ei brawd, clymu llythyr dan ei hadain, a’i hanfon dros y môr. Cafodd hi Fendigeidfran yng Nghaer Seint yn Arfon, a daeth holl lu Prydain i’w hachub. Pan ddaeth y rhyfel ofnadwy i ben a dim ond saith yn dod adref gyda hi, glaniodd yn Aber Alaw ym Môn, edrych yn ôl ar Iwerddon ac ar Brydain, a dweud: ‘Gwae fi o’m ganedigaeth. Da dwy ynys a ddiffeithwyd o’m hachos i.’ Torrodd ei chalon, a’i chladdu mewn bedd pedairochrog ar lan Alaw.",
    "Mae Bedd Branwen, carnedd o Oes yr Efydd ger Llanddeusant ym Môn, wedi dwyn ei henw ers canrifoedd. Saif Branwen dros bob gwraig a dalodd bris cwerylon pobl eraill, ac mae ei drudwen yn un o’r delweddau anwylaf yn llenyddiaeth Gymraeg."]),
 P("manawydan", "ancient", D4, "Manawydan fab Llŷr", "Manawydan fab Llŷr", "The patient prince, hero of the Third Branch", "Y tywysog amyneddgar, arwr y Drydedd Gainc", "Manawydan", "rulers",
   "Brother of Bendigeidfran and Branwen, one of the seven who came home from Ireland, and the hero of the Third Branch, who won back the whole of Dyfed with his wits and his patience.",
   "Brawd Bendigeidfran a Branwen, un o’r saith a ddaeth adref o Iwerddon, ac arwr y Drydedd Gainc, a enillodd Ddyfed gyfan yn ôl â’i synnwyr a’i amynedd.",
   ["Manawydan son of Llŷr was the brother of Bendigeidfran and Branwen. In the Second Branch he sat beside his brother on the rock of Harlech, crossed to Ireland with the host, and was one of the seven who carried home the head of Brân. In the Third Branch, which bears his name, he is the hero.",
    "Left without land when his cousin Caswallon took the island, he went with his friend Pryderi to Dyfed and married Pryderi’s mother, Rhiannon. When an enchantment emptied the land, Manawydan led the little company to England and earned their bread by his own hands, as a saddler, a shield-maker and a shoemaker, so skilled that the craftsmen of each town plotted against him. Every time, he refused to fight and moved on. When Pryderi and Rhiannon were lost in a vanishing fort, he swore to be a true friend to Pryderi’s wife Cigfa, and kept his word.",
    "In the end he caught a mouse in his wheat field and would not let it go, for any price, until the bishop who came for it confessed that he was Llwyd son of Cil Coed, the enchanter of Dyfed. Manawydan’s price was the freedom of Rhiannon and Pryderi, the lifting of the spell, and a promise of no more revenge. So he won back a whole kingdom without striking a blow.",
    "The Triads name him one of the Three Humble Chieftains of the Island of Britain, because he never sought land for himself, and one of the Three Golden Shoemakers. His name is linked with Manaw, the Isle of Man. He is the wise hero of the Mabinogi: patient, courteous and clever, and never ashamed to work with his hands."],
   ["Roedd Manawydan fab Llŷr yn frawd i Fendigeidfran a Branwen. Yn yr Ail Gainc eisteddai wrth ochr ei frawd ar graig Harlech, croesodd i Iwerddon gyda’r llu, ac roedd yn un o’r saith a gludodd ben Brân adref. Yn y Drydedd Gainc, sy’n dwyn ei enw, ef yw’r arwr.",
    "Wedi’i adael heb dir pan gymerodd ei gefnder Caswallon yr ynys, aeth gyda’i gyfaill Pryderi i Ddyfed a phriodi mam Pryderi, Rhiannon. Pan wagiodd hud y wlad, arweiniodd Manawydan y cwmni bach i Loegr ac ennill eu bara â’i ddwylo ei hun, yn gyfrwywr, yn wneuthurwr tarianau ac yn grydd, mor fedrus nes i grefftwyr pob tref gynllwynio yn ei erbyn. Bob tro, gwrthododd ymladd ac aeth ymlaen. Pan gollwyd Pryderi a Rhiannon mewn caer a ddiflannodd, tyngodd y byddai’n gyfaill cywir i wraig Pryderi, Cigfa, a chadwodd ei air.",
    "Yn y diwedd daliodd lygoden yn ei gae gwenith ac ni fynnai ei gollwng, am unrhyw bris, nes i’r esgob a ddaeth i’w cheisio gyfaddef mai Llwyd fab Cil Coed ydoedd, swynwr Dyfed. Pris Manawydan oedd rhyddid Rhiannon a Phryderi, codi’r hud, ac addewid na fyddai rhagor o ddial. Felly enillodd deyrnas gyfan yn ôl heb daro’r un ergyd.",
    "Mae’r Trioedd yn ei enwi’n un o Dri Lleddf Unben Ynys Prydain, am na cheisiodd dir iddo’i hun erioed, ac yn un o’r Tri Eurgrydd. Cysylltir ei enw â Manaw, Ynys Manaw. Ef yw arwr doeth y Mabinogi: amyneddgar, cwrtais a chlyfar, a byth â chywilydd o weithio â’i ddwylo."]),
 P("matholwch", "ancient", D4, "Matholwch", "Matholwch", "King of Ireland in the Second Branch", "Brenin Iwerddon yn yr Ail Gainc", "Matholwch", "rulers",
   "The king of Ireland who sailed to Harlech to ask for Branwen, received the cauldron of rebirth, and was drawn into the war between the two islands.",
   "Brenin Iwerddon a hwyliodd i Harlech i ofyn am Franwen, a gafodd y pair dadeni, ac a dynnwyd i’r rhyfel rhwng y ddwy ynys.",
   ["Matholwch, king of Ireland, came with thirteen ships to Harlech to ask Bendigeidfran for his sister Branwen, so that the Island of the Mighty and Ireland would be joined in friendship. The wedding was held at Aberffraw. When Efnysien maimed his horses, Bendigeidfran made full amends with horses, a staff of silver, a plate of gold and the Pair Dadeni, the cauldron of rebirth.",
    "In Ireland, under pressure from his own people, he let Branwen be punished for an insult that was not hers. When the host of Britain came, he offered peace, a great house for Bendigeidfran, and the kingdom of Ireland for his son Gwern, Branwen’s son. But the peace was broken in the hall, and the war that followed left Ireland and Britain both laid waste."],
   ["Daeth Matholwch, brenin Iwerddon, â thair llong ar ddeg i Harlech i ofyn i Fendigeidfran am ei chwaer Branwen, fel yr unid Ynys y Cedyrn ac Iwerddon mewn cyfeillgarwch. Cynhaliwyd y briodas yn Aberffraw. Pan anafodd Efnysien ei feirch, gwnaeth Bendigeidfran iawn llawn â meirch, ffon arian, plât aur a’r Pair Dadeni.",
    "Yn Iwerddon, dan bwysau ei bobl ei hun, gadawodd i Franwen gael ei chosbi am sarhad nad oedd yn eiddo iddi hi. Pan ddaeth llu Prydain, cynigiodd hedd, tŷ mawr i Fendigeidfran, a theyrnas Iwerddon i’w fab Gwern, mab Branwen. Ond torrwyd yr hedd yn y neuadd, a gadawodd y rhyfel a ddilynodd Iwerddon a Phrydain ill dwy yn ddiffaith."]),
 P("efnysien", "ancient", D4, "Efnysien", "Efnysien", "Half-brother of Branwen, who broke the cauldron", "Hanner brawd Branwen, a dorrodd y pair", "Efnysien", "heroes",
   "Half-brother of Bendigeidfran and Branwen, a man who could set two brothers fighting. In the end he gave his life to break the cauldron of rebirth and save the host of Britain.",
   "Hanner brawd Bendigeidfran a Branwen, gŵr a allai beri i ddau frawd ymladd. Yn y diwedd rhoddodd ei fywyd i dorri’r pair dadeni ac achub llu Prydain.",
   ["Efnysien and his twin Nisien were the sons of Penarddun, mother of Branwen, by Euroswydd. Nisien could make peace between two armies at their angriest; Efnysien could set two brothers fighting at their most loving. He is the stormy spirit of the Second Branch. Angry that his sister had been given away without his word, he maimed the horses of the king of Ireland, and it was his anger that began the quarrel between the two islands.",
    "When the Irish hid two hundred armed men in bags on the pillars of the great house, it was Efnysien who found them and crushed them, saving Bendigeidfran’s host from ambush. And when he saw the Irish dead rising again from the cauldron while the men of Britain could not, he was sorry for all he had done. He hid among the Irish dead, was thrown into the cauldron, and stretched himself until it burst into four pieces, and his heart burst with it. That sacrifice gave the host of Britain its victory."],
   ["Meibion Penarddun, mam Branwen, o Euroswydd oedd Efnysien a’i efaill Nisien. Gallai Nisien wneud hedd rhwng dwy fyddin ar eu llidiocaf; gallai Efnysien beri i ddau frawd ymladd ar eu mwyaf cariadus. Ef yw ysbryd tymhestlog yr Ail Gainc. Yn ddig i’w chwaer gael ei rhoi heb ei air ef, anafodd feirch brenin Iwerddon, a’i ddicter ef a gychwynnodd y cweryl rhwng y ddwy ynys.",
    "Pan guddiodd y Gwyddyl ddau gant o wŷr arfog mewn boliau ar golofnau’r tŷ mawr, Efnysien a’u cafodd a’u gwasgu, gan achub llu Bendigeidfran rhag cynllwyn. A phan welodd feirw’r Gwyddyl yn codi eto o’r pair a gwŷr Prydain heb allu, bu’n edifar am bopeth a wnaethai. Ymguddiodd ymhlith meirw’r Gwyddyl, fe’i taflwyd i’r pair, ac ymestynnodd nes iddo dorri’n bedwar darn, a thorrodd ei galon yntau. Rhoddodd yr aberth hwnnw’r fuddugoliaeth i lu Prydain."]),

 # ---------- the Fourth Branch ----------
 P("math", "ancient", D4, "Math fab Mathonwy", "Math fab Mathonwy", "Lord of Gwynedd, the master of magic", "Arglwydd Gwynedd, meistr yr hud", "Math_fab_Mathonwy", "rulers",
   "Lord of Gwynedd in the Fourth Branch, the greatest enchanter of the Mabinogi, who could hear any whisper the wind carried. With Gwydion he made Blodeuwedd out of flowers.",
   "Arglwydd Gwynedd yn y Bedwaredd Gainc, swynwr mwyaf y Mabinogi, a glywai unrhyw sibrwd a gludai’r gwynt. Gyda Gwydion gwnaeth Flodeuwedd o flodau.",
   ["Math son of Mathonwy was lord over Gwynedd, with his court at Caer Dathyl in Arfon. He was the greatest master of magic in the Four Branches. The Triads name the Three Great Enchantments of the Island of Britain, and the first of them is the enchantment of Math son of Mathonwy, which he taught to his nephew Gwydion. The tale says that whatever whisper was spoken, however quietly, if the wind met it, Math would know it.",
    "He could not live, the tale tells, unless his feet lay in the lap of a maiden, except in time of war. When his nephew Gilfaethwy wronged Goewin, his footholder, Math married her to make amends and gave her authority over his kingdom, and he punished his nephews by turning them into pairs of animals for three years. Then, with justice done, he forgave them.",
    "When Arianrhod laid her three destinies on her son Lleu, Math and Gwydion together made him a wife out of the flowers of the oak, the broom and the meadowsweet, and named her Blodeuwedd. Math gave Lleu the cantref of Dinoding to rule. He is the wise old king of the Fourth Branch: stern in judgement, but just, and ready to forgive."],
   ["Math fab Mathonwy oedd arglwydd Gwynedd, a’i lys yng Nghaer Dathyl yn Arfon. Ef oedd meistr mwyaf hud yn y Pedair Cainc. Mae’r Trioedd yn enwi Tri Phrif Hud Ynys Prydain, a’r cyntaf ohonynt yw hud Math fab Mathonwy, a ddysgodd i’w nai Gwydion. Dywed y chwedl, pa sibrwd bynnag a ddywedid, pa mor dawel bynnag, os cyfarfyddai’r gwynt ag ef, y gwyddai Math amdano.",
    "Ni allai fyw, medd y chwedl, oni bai fod ei draed yng nghôl morwyn, oddieithr adeg rhyfel. Pan wnaeth ei nai Gilfaethwy gam â Goewin, ei forwyn droed, priododd Math hi i wneud iawn a rhoi iddi awdurdod ar ei deyrnas, a chosbodd ei neiaint drwy eu troi’n barau o anifeiliaid am dair blynedd. Yna, wedi gwneud cyfiawnder, maddeuodd iddynt.",
    "Pan dyngodd Arianrhod ei thair tynged ar ei mab Lleu, gwnaeth Math a Gwydion gyda’i gilydd wraig iddo o flodau’r dderwen, y banadl a’r erwain, a’i henwi’n Flodeuwedd. Rhoddodd Math gantref Dinoding i Leu i’w lywodraethu. Ef yw hen frenin doeth y Bedwaredd Gainc: llym ei farn, ond cyfiawn, ac yn barod i faddau."]),
 P("gwydion", "ancient", D4, "Gwydion fab Dôn", "Gwydion fab Dôn", "Enchanter and the best storyteller in the world", "Swynwr a chyfarwydd gorau’r byd", "Gwydion", "words",
   "Son of Dôn and nephew of Math, the best storyteller in the world and a master of magic. He raised Lleu, won him a name and arms, made Blodeuwedd from flowers, and brought Lleu back from the shape of an eagle.",
   "Mab Dôn a nai Math, cyfarwydd gorau’r byd a meistr hud. Magodd Leu, enillodd enw ac arfau iddo, gwnaeth Flodeuwedd o flodau, a dwyn Lleu yn ôl o rith eryr.",
   ["Gwydion son of Dôn is the great enchanter of the Fourth Branch, and the tale says he was the best teller of tales in the world. When he came to Pryderi’s court disguised as a bard, he entertained the whole court so well that everyone there loved him. He learned his magic from his uncle Math, and the Triads count Math’s enchantment, which he taught to Gwydion, among the Three Great Enchantments of the Island of Britain.",
    "His story has its dark side. To help his brother Gilfaethwy, he tricked Pryderi out of the swine of Annwn with horses and hounds conjured out of toadstools, started a war, and killed Pryderi in single combat at Y Felenrhyd. Math punished him for three years in the shapes of a stag, a boar and a wolf. But the rest of his story is one of love and loyalty. He saved his sister Arianrhod’s little son, raised him, and outwitted her three destinies: by his cunning the boy won his name, Lleu Llaw Gyffes, and his arms, and with Math, Gwydion made him a wife of flowers.",
    "When Lleu was struck down and flew off as an eagle, it was Gwydion who searched the whole of Gwynedd and Powys for him, followed a sow to the oak in Nantlle, and sang the three englynion that brought the eagle down to his knee. Those englynion, among the oldest verses in Welsh, are still read today. In old Welsh the Milky Way was called Caer Gwydion, Gwydion’s fortress."],
   ["Gwydion fab Dôn yw swynwr mawr y Bedwaredd Gainc, a dywed y chwedl mai ef oedd cyfarwydd gorau’r byd. Pan ddaeth i lys Pryderi yn rhith bardd, diddanodd y llys cyfan mor dda nes i bawb yno ei garu. Dysgodd ei hud gan ei ewythr Math, ac mae’r Trioedd yn cyfrif hud Math, a ddysgodd i Wydion, ymhlith Tri Phrif Hud Ynys Prydain.",
    "Mae ochr dywyll i’w hanes. I helpu ei frawd Gilfaethwy, twyllodd Bryderi o foch Annwn â meirch a chŵn a swynwyd o fadarch, cychwynnodd ryfel, a lladdodd Bryderi mewn ymladdfa un-ac-un yn y Felenrhyd. Cosbodd Math ef am dair blynedd ym mhryd carw, baedd a blaidd. Ond hanes cariad a ffyddlondeb yw gweddill ei stori. Achubodd fab bach ei chwaer Arianrhod, ei fagu, a threchu ei thair tynged: drwy ei gyfrwystra enillodd y bachgen ei enw, Lleu Llaw Gyffes, a’i arfau, a chyda Math gwnaeth Gwydion wraig o flodau iddo.",
    "Pan drawyd Lleu a hedfan ymaith yn eryr, Gwydion a chwiliodd Wynedd a Phowys i gyd amdano, dilyn hwch i’r dderwen yn Nantlle, a chanu’r tri englyn a ddaeth â’r eryr i lawr i’w lin. Mae’r englynion hynny, ymhlith yr hynaf o benillion y Gymraeg, yn cael eu darllen heddiw. Yn yr hen Gymraeg Caer Gwydion oedd enw’r Llwybr Llaethog."]),
 P("gilfaethwy", "ancient", D4, "Gilfaethwy fab Dôn", "Gilfaethwy fab Dôn", "Brother of Gwydion", "Brawd Gwydion", "Gilfaethwy", "heroes",
   "Son of Dôn and brother of Gwydion. His love for Goewin set off the war between Gwynedd and the south, and Math punished him and his brother in the shapes of beasts.",
   "Mab Dôn a brawd Gwydion. Cychwynnodd ei gariad at Goewin y rhyfel rhwng Gwynedd a’r de, a chosbodd Math ef a’i frawd ym mhryd anifeiliaid.",
   ["Gilfaethwy son of Dôn, brother of Gwydion and nephew of Math, fell sick with love for Goewin, his uncle’s footholder, at the start of the Fourth Branch. Gwydion started a war with Pryderi to draw Math away from his court, and while Math was at war, Gilfaethwy took Goewin by force.",
    "Math’s punishment was to turn him and Gwydion into a stag and a hind, a boar and a sow, and a wolf and a she-wolf, one year after another, so that they shared the life of the wild beasts and bore young together. After three years Math gave them back their own shapes and forgave them, and the tale ends their story there."],
   ["Aeth Gilfaethwy fab Dôn, brawd Gwydion a nai Math, yn glaf o gariad at Goewin, morwyn droed ei ewythr, ar ddechrau’r Bedwaredd Gainc. Cychwynnodd Gwydion ryfel â Phryderi i dynnu Math ymaith o’i lys, a thra oedd Math yn y rhyfel, treisiodd Gilfaethwy Goewin.",
    "Cosb Math oedd eu troi ef a Gwydion yn garw ac ewig, yn faedd a hwch, ac yn flaidd a bleiddast, flwyddyn ar ôl blwyddyn, fel y rhannent fywyd yr anifeiliaid gwyllt a magu rhai bach gyda’i gilydd. Wedi tair blynedd rhoddodd Math eu pryd eu hunain yn ôl iddynt a maddau iddynt, ac mae’r chwedl yn gorffen eu hanes yno."]),
 P("goewin", "ancient", D4, "Goewin ferch Pebin", "Goewin ferch Pebin", "Queen of Gwynedd, wife of Math", "Brenhines Gwynedd, gwraig Math", "Goewin", "rulers",
   "The fairest maiden of her time, Math’s footholder at Caer Dathyl. Wronged by Gilfaethwy, she spoke out, and Math made her his queen with authority over his kingdom.",
   "Morwyn decaf ei hoes, morwyn droed Math yng Nghaer Dathyl. Wedi i Gilfaethwy wneud cam â hi, siaradodd yn blaen, a gwnaeth Math hi’n frenhines iddo ag awdurdod ar ei deyrnas.",
   ["Goewin, daughter of Pebin of Dôl Pebin in Arfon, was the fairest maiden of her time, and held the feet of Math, lord of Gwynedd, in her lap at his court of Caer Dathyl. When Math was away at war, Gilfaethwy son of Dôn took her by force.",
    "When Math came home, Goewin told him the truth plainly and with courage. ‘Lord,’ she said, ‘seek a maiden to be your footholder, for I am a woman.’ Math did right by her. He made her his wife, to make amends for the wrong done to her, and gave her authority over his kingdom, and he punished the brothers who had wronged her. Goewin is remembered for her honesty and dignity, and for the justice she won."],
   ["Goewin ferch Pebin o Ddôl Bebin yn Arfon oedd morwyn decaf ei hoes, a daliai draed Math, arglwydd Gwynedd, yn ei chôl yn ei lys yng Nghaer Dathyl. Tra oedd Math i ffwrdd yn y rhyfel, treisiodd Gilfaethwy fab Dôn hi.",
    "Pan ddaeth Math adref, dywedodd Goewin y gwir wrtho’n blaen ac yn ddewr. ‘Arglwydd,’ meddai, ‘ceisia forwyn i fod yn forwyn droed i ti, canys gwraig wyf i.’ Gwnaeth Math yn iawn â hi. Fe’i gwnaeth yn wraig iddo, i wneud iawn am y cam a wnaed iddi, a rhoi iddi awdurdod ar ei deyrnas, a chosbodd y brodyr a wnaethai gam â hi. Cofir Goewin am ei gonestrwydd a’i hurddas, ac am y cyfiawnder a enillodd."]),
 P("arianrhod", "ancient", D4, "Arianrhod ferch Dôn", "Arianrhod ferch Dôn", "Lady of Caer Arianrhod, mother of Lleu", "Arglwyddes Caer Arianrhod, mam Lleu", "Arianrhod", "rulers",
   "Daughter of Dôn and sister of Gwydion, lady of the sea fort of Caer Arianrhod, mother of Dylan and Lleu. Her name, ‘silver wheel’, was given to the stars of the Northern Crown.",
   "Merch Dôn a chwaer Gwydion, arglwyddes caer fôr Caer Arianrhod, mam Dylan a Lleu. Rhoddwyd ei henw, ‘yr olwyn arian’, i sêr y Goron Ogleddol.",
   ["Arianrhod, daughter of Dôn, is one of the great figures of the Fourth Branch. Her name means ‘silver wheel’, and she ruled her own court, Caer Arianrhod, by the sea on the coast of Arfon. When Math needed a new footholder and asked her to step over his magic wand, she gave birth to two sons: Dylan, who went straight to the sea, and a little child whom her brother Gwydion hid and raised.",
    "Angry at being shamed before the court, she laid three destinies on the boy: that he would have no name unless she gave it, no arms unless she armed him, and no wife of any race on earth. Gwydion outwitted the first two, and so she named her son herself, Lleu Llaw Gyffes, and armed him herself. The third destiny led to the making of Blodeuwedd.",
    "Off the coast at Dinas Dinlle, a reef that shows at low tide is still called Caer Arianrhod, and the old tale of the drowned court of Arianrhod is still told in Arfon. In old Welsh the constellation of the Northern Crown, the Corona Borealis, was called Caer Arianrhod. She is a proud, powerful and independent lady, who would not be told what to do."],
   ["Mae Arianrhod ferch Dôn yn un o ffigurau mawr y Bedwaredd Gainc. Ystyr ei henw yw ‘olwyn arian’, a llywodraethai ei llys ei hun, Caer Arianrhod, ar lan y môr ar arfordir Arfon. Pan oedd ar Fath angen morwyn droed newydd a gofyn iddi gamu dros ei hudlath, esgorodd ar ddau fab: Dylan, a aeth yn syth i’r môr, a phlentyn bach a guddiodd a magodd ei brawd Gwydion.",
    "Yn ddig am gael ei chywilyddio gerbron y llys, tyngodd dair tynged ar y bachgen: na châi enw oni roddai hi ef, nac arfau oni bai iddi hi ei arfogi, na gwraig o’r un genedl ar y ddaear. Trechodd Gwydion y ddwy gyntaf, ac felly hi ei hun a enwodd ei mab, Lleu Llaw Gyffes, a hi ei hun a’i harfogodd. Arweiniodd y drydedd dynged at wneud Blodeuwedd.",
    "Oddi ar yr arfordir yn Ninas Dinlle, gelwir craig a ddengys ar drai yn Gaer Arianrhod o hyd, ac adroddir yr hen chwedl am lys boddedig Arianrhod yn Arfon o hyd. Yn yr hen Gymraeg Caer Arianrhod oedd enw cytser y Goron Ogleddol. Mae hi’n arglwyddes falch, rymus ac annibynnol, na fynnai i neb ddweud wrthi beth i’w wneud."]),
 P("dylan-ail-don", "ancient", D4, "Dylan Eil Ton", "Dylan Eil Ton", "Son of the Wave", "Mab y Don", "Dylan_ail_Don", "heroes",
   "Son of Arianrhod, who made for the sea as soon as he was born and swam like a fish. No wave ever broke beneath him, and the sea mourned him.",
   "Mab Arianrhod, a aeth am y môr cyn gynted ag y’i ganed a nofio fel pysgodyn. Ni thorrodd ton dano erioed, a galarodd y môr amdano.",
   ["Dylan was the first son born to Arianrhod in the Fourth Branch, a fine, sturdy, yellow-haired boy. Math baptised him, and the moment he was baptised he made for the sea, took on the nature of the sea, and swam as well as the best fish in it. He was called Dylan Eil Ton, Dylan Son of the Wave, because no wave ever broke beneath him.",
    "The tale says he was killed by a blow from his uncle Gofannon the smith, one of the Three Unfortunate Blows of the Island of Britain. Poems in the Book of Taliesin mourn him, and say that the waves of Ireland, Man, the North and Britain wept for him. The sound of the sea breaking on the shore, it was said, was the waves longing for him. Dylan Thomas was named after him."],
   ["Dylan oedd y mab cyntaf a aned i Arianrhod yn y Bedwaredd Gainc, bachgen braf, cadarn, melyn ei wallt. Bedyddiodd Math ef, a’r eiliad y bedyddiwyd ef aeth am y môr, cymryd anian y môr, a nofio cystal â’r pysgodyn gorau ynddo. Fe’i galwyd yn Ddylan Eil Ton, am na thorrodd ton dano erioed.",
    "Dywed y chwedl iddo gael ei ladd gan ergyd gan ei ewythr Gofannon y gof, un o Dri Anfad Ddyrnod Ynys Prydain. Mae cerddi yn Llyfr Taliesin yn galaru amdano, ac yn dweud i donnau Iwerddon, Manaw, y Gogledd a Phrydain wylo amdano. Sŵn y môr yn torri ar y lan, meddid, oedd y tonnau’n hiraethu amdano. Ar ei ôl ef yr enwyd Dylan Thomas."]),
 P("lleu", "ancient", D4, "Lleu Llaw Gyffes", "Lleu Llaw Gyffes", "Lord of Gwynedd, Lleu of the Skilful Hand", "Arglwydd Gwynedd, Lleu â’r llaw gyffes", "Lleu_Llaw_Gyffes", "rulers",
   "Son of Arianrhod, raised by Gwydion, who won his name and his arms against his mother’s destinies, married Blodeuwedd, was struck down and became an eagle, and came back to rule Gwynedd.",
   "Mab Arianrhod, a fagwyd gan Wydion, a enillodd ei enw a’i arfau yn erbyn tynghedau ei fam, a briododd Flodeuwedd, a drawyd ac a aeth yn eryr, ac a ddaeth yn ôl i lywodraethu Gwynedd.",
   ["Lleu Llaw Gyffes is the young hero of the Fourth Branch. Born to Arianrhod as a small, unformed thing, he was hidden in a chest by his uncle Gwydion and raised by him, growing twice as fast as other children. His mother swore he would never have a name, arms or a wife unless she gave them. Gwydion won him the first two by cunning: when the boy hit a wren with a single throw, his mother herself laughed, ‘The fair one has hit it with a skilful hand,’ and so he was Lleu Llaw Gyffes, Lleu of the Skilful Hand. For a wife, Math and Gwydion made him Blodeuwedd out of flowers.",
    "Math gave him the cantref of Dinoding, and he held court at Mur Castell in Ardudwy, the Roman fort of Tomen y Mur. There Blodeuwedd and Gronw Pebr plotted his death. Lleu could be killed only in one strange way, standing with one foot on a bath by a river and the other on a goat. Tricked into showing how, he was struck by Gronw’s spear, gave a terrible scream and flew off in the shape of an eagle.",
    "Gwydion found him at last in an oak in Nantlle, sang him down with three englynion, and restored him. Lleu was healed, and he took his revenge on Gronw with a spear thrown through the stone that Gronw held before him, Llech Ronw on the Cynfal. He won back his lands, and became lord of all Gwynedd.",
    "His name is the Welsh form of Lugus, honoured across the land of the Britons and beyond: the city of Lyon was Lugdunum, ‘the fort of Lugus’. Dinas Dinlle on the coast of Arfon is Dinas Dinlleu, Lleu’s fort, and Nantlle is Lleu’s valley."],
   ["Lleu Llaw Gyffes yw arwr ifanc y Bedwaredd Gainc. Wedi’i eni i Arianrhod yn beth bychan, anffurf, fe’i cuddiwyd mewn cist gan ei ewythr Gwydion a’i fagu ganddo, gan dyfu ddwywaith cyn gyflymed â phlant eraill. Tyngodd ei fam na châi byth enw, arfau na gwraig oni roddai hi hwy. Enillodd Gwydion y ddau gyntaf iddo drwy gyfrwystra: pan drawodd y bachgen ddryw ag un ergyd, chwarddodd ei fam ei hun, ‘Dioer, â llaw gyffes y medrodd y lleu ef,’ ac felly Lleu Llaw Gyffes oedd ef. Yn wraig, gwnaeth Math a Gwydion Flodeuwedd iddo o flodau.",
    "Rhoddodd Math gantref Dinoding iddo, a chynhaliai ei lys ym Mur Castell yn Ardudwy, caer Rufeinig Tomen y Mur. Yno cynllwyniodd Blodeuwedd a Gronw Pebr ei farwolaeth. Dim ond mewn un ffordd ryfedd y gellid lladd Lleu, yn sefyll ag un droed ar faddon ger afon a’r llall ar fwch. Wedi’i dwyllo i ddangos sut, trawyd ef gan waywffon Gronw, rhoddodd sgrech ofnadwy a hedfan ymaith yn rhith eryr.",
    "Cafodd Gwydion ef o’r diwedd mewn derwen yn Nantlle, ei ganu i lawr â thri englyn, a’i adfer. Iachawyd Lleu, a dialodd ar Ronw â gwaywffon a daflwyd drwy’r llech a ddaliai Gronw o’i flaen, Llech Ronw ar afon Cynfal. Enillodd ei diroedd yn ôl, a dod yn arglwydd ar Wynedd gyfan.",
    "Ffurf Gymraeg ar Lugus yw ei enw, duw a anrhydeddwyd ar draws gwlad y Brythoniaid a thu hwnt: Lugdunum, ‘caer Lugus’, oedd dinas Lyon. Dinas Dinlleu, caer Lleu, yw Dinas Dinlle ar arfordir Arfon, a Nantlle yw cwm Lleu."]),
 P("blodeuwedd", "ancient", D4, "Blodeuwedd", "Blodeuwedd", "The woman made of flowers", "Y ferch a wnaed o flodau", "Blodeuwedd", "heroes",
   "Made by Math and Gwydion from the flowers of the oak, the broom and the meadowsweet to be Lleu’s wife. She loved Gronw Pebr, and was turned into an owl, which still bears her name in Welsh.",
   "Fe’i gwnaed gan Fath a Gwydion o flodau’r dderwen, y banadl a’r erwain i fod yn wraig i Leu. Carodd Ronw Pebr, a’i throi’n dylluan, sy’n dal i ddwyn ei henw yn Gymraeg.",
   ["Blodeuwedd, ‘Flower-face’, is the most famous figure of the Fourth Branch. Arianrhod had sworn that her son Lleu would never have a wife of any race on earth, so Math and Gwydion took the flowers of the oak, the broom and the meadowsweet, and from them they conjured the fairest maiden anyone had ever seen. She was married to Lleu, and they lived at his court of Mur Castell in Ardudwy.",
    "But she was never asked what she wanted. When Gronw Pebr, lord of Penllyn, rode by after a stag, she invited him in for courtesy’s sake, and the moment they looked at each other they were filled with love. Together they plotted Lleu’s death, and she drew from him the secret of how he could be killed. When Lleu came back from the shape of an eagle, she fled with her maidens over the mountain. The maidens drowned in Llyn y Morwynion, and Gwydion turned Blodeuwedd into an owl, never to show her face by day. To this day blodeuwedd is a Welsh word for the owl.",
    "Her story has moved writers more than any other in the Mabinogi. Saunders Lewis made her the heroine of his great verse play Blodeuwedd, a woman made by men for a man, longing for a life of her own. Alan Garner retold her story in The Owl Service, set in a Welsh valley, which won the Carnegie Medal in 1967. Every generation reads her afresh."],
   ["Blodeuwedd yw ffigur enwocaf y Bedwaredd Gainc. Roedd Arianrhod wedi tyngu na châi ei mab Lleu byth wraig o’r un genedl ar y ddaear, felly cymerodd Math a Gwydion flodau’r dderwen, y banadl a’r erwain, ac ohonynt swyno’r forwyn decaf a welodd neb erioed. Fe’i priodwyd â Lleu, a buont yn byw yn ei lys ym Mur Castell yn Ardudwy.",
    "Ond ni ofynnwyd iddi erioed beth a fynnai hi. Pan farchogodd Gronw Pebr, arglwydd Penllyn, heibio ar ôl carw, gwahoddodd hi ef i mewn er cwrteisi, a’r eiliad yr edrychasant ar ei gilydd fe’u llanwyd â chariad. Gyda’i gilydd cynllwyniasant farwolaeth Lleu, a chafodd hi ganddo’r gyfrinach sut y gellid ei ladd. Pan ddaeth Lleu yn ôl o rith eryr, ffodd gyda’i morynion dros y mynydd. Boddodd y morynion yn Llyn y Morwynion, a throdd Gwydion Flodeuwedd yn dylluan, na chaiff byth ddangos ei hwyneb liw dydd. Hyd heddiw mae blodeuwedd yn air Cymraeg am y dylluan.",
    "Mae ei hanes wedi cyffwrdd ag awduron yn fwy nag unrhyw un arall yn y Mabinogi. Gwnaeth Saunders Lewis hi’n arwres ei ddrama fydryddol fawr Blodeuwedd, gwraig a wnaed gan ddynion i ddyn, yn hiraethu am fywyd ei hun. Ailadroddodd Alan Garner ei hanes yn The Owl Service, wedi’i gosod mewn cwm yng Nghymru, a enillodd Fedal Carnegie yn 1967. Mae pob cenhedlaeth yn ei darllen o’r newydd."]),
 P("gronw-pebr", "ancient", D4, "Gronw Pebr", "Gronw Pebr", "Lord of Penllyn", "Arglwydd Penllyn", "Gronw_Pebr", "rulers",
   "Lord of Penllyn, who loved Blodeuwedd and struck Lleu with the spear. He met his end behind the stone by the Cynfal still called Llech Ronw.",
   "Arglwydd Penllyn, a garodd Flodeuwedd ac a drawodd Leu â’r waywffon. Daeth ei ddiwedd y tu ôl i’r llech ger afon Cynfal a elwir yn Llech Ronw o hyd.",
   ["Gronw Pebr was lord of Penllyn, the country around Llyn Tegid and Bala. Hunting a stag down to the river Cynfal in Ardudwy, he was invited into the court of Mur Castell by Blodeuwedd, wife of Lleu, and the two fell in love at first sight. Together they plotted the death of Lleu, and a year later, from behind the hill called Bryn Cyfergyr, Gronw threw the spear that struck him down.",
    "When Lleu came back, Gronw offered land and gold to make amends, and when Lleu would take nothing but a blow in return, Gronw asked his own men if any would take the blow for him. None would, and the Triads remember his war band as one of the Three Disloyal War Bands of the Island of Britain. Gronw faced the blow himself, asking only to hold a stone between himself and the spear. Lleu’s spear went through the stone and through him. The stone, with its hole, is called Llech Ronw, on the bank of the Cynfal."],
   ["Gronw Pebr oedd arglwydd Penllyn, y wlad o gwmpas Llyn Tegid a’r Bala. Wrth hela carw i lawr at afon Cynfal yn Ardudwy, fe’i gwahoddwyd i mewn i lys Mur Castell gan Flodeuwedd, gwraig Lleu, a syrthiodd y ddau mewn cariad ar yr olwg gyntaf. Gyda’i gilydd cynllwyniasant farwolaeth Lleu, a blwyddyn yn ddiweddarach, o’r tu ôl i’r bryn a elwir Bryn Cyfergyr, taflodd Gronw’r waywffon a’i trawodd.",
    "Pan ddaeth Lleu yn ôl, cynigiodd Gronw dir ac aur i wneud iawn, a phan na chymerai Lleu ddim ond ergyd yn ôl, gofynnodd Gronw i’w wŷr ei hun a gymerai un ohonynt yr ergyd drosto. Ni fynnai neb, ac mae’r Trioedd yn cofio ei osgordd fel un o Dri Anniwair Deulu Ynys Prydain. Wynebodd Gronw’r ergyd ei hun, gan ofyn yn unig am gael dal llech rhyngddo a’r waywffon. Aeth gwaywffon Lleu drwy’r llech a thrwyddo yntau. Llech Ronw yw enw’r llech, â’i thwll, ar lan afon Cynfal."]),

 # ---------- the people who kept the tales ----------
 P("rhydderch-ab-ieuan-llwyd", "medieval", "c. 1325–c. 1400", "Rhydderch ab Ieuan Llwyd", "Rhydderch ab Ieuan Llwyd", "Patron of the White Book of Rhydderch", "Noddwr Llyfr Gwyn Rhydderch", "Rhydderch_ab_Ieuan_Llwyd", "learning",
   "Gentleman, lawyer and patron of Parcrhydderch in Ceredigion, for whom the White Book of Rhydderch, the oldest complete book of the Mabinogi tales, was written about 1350.",
   "Uchelwr, cyfreithiwr a noddwr o Barcrhydderch yng Ngheredigion, yr ysgrifennwyd Llyfr Gwyn Rhydderch iddo tua 1350, y llyfr cyflawn hynaf o chwedlau’r Mabinogi.",
   ["Rhydderch ab Ieuan Llwyd lived at Parcrhydderch in the parish of Llangeitho in Ceredigion, in the fourteenth century. He came of a family of patrons of the poets in the valley of the Aeron, was learned in the law of Hywel Dda, and was one of the most cultured Welshmen of his day. The poets sang his praise.",
    "About 1350 a team of five scribes, probably working at the abbey of Strata Florida, made a great book for him: the White Book of Rhydderch, Llyfr Gwyn Rhydderch. It holds the oldest complete copy of most of the tales of the Mabinogion, with poetry, the lives of saints and the stories of Charlemagne in Welsh. The book is in the National Library of Wales today, as Peniarth MSS 4 and 5. Every reader of the Four Branches owes him a debt: without the book he paid for, much of the Mabinogi might be lost."],
   ["Roedd Rhydderch ab Ieuan Llwyd yn byw ym Mharcrhydderch ym mhlwyf Llangeitho yng Ngheredigion, yn y bedwaredd ganrif ar ddeg. Hanai o deulu o noddwyr beirdd yn Nyffryn Aeron, roedd yn hyddysg yng nghyfraith Hywel Dda, ac roedd yn un o Gymry mwyaf diwylliedig ei ddydd. Canodd y beirdd ei glod.",
    "Tua 1350 lluniodd tîm o bump o gopïwyr, yn gweithio yn abaty Ystrad Fflur yn ôl pob tebyg, lyfr mawr iddo: Llyfr Gwyn Rhydderch. Ynddo ceir y copi cyflawn hynaf o’r rhan fwyaf o chwedlau’r Mabinogion, gyda barddoniaeth, bucheddau saint a straeon Siarlymaen yn Gymraeg. Mae’r llyfr yn Llyfrgell Genedlaethol Cymru heddiw, fel Llawysgrifau Peniarth 4 a 5. Mae pob darllenydd y Pedair Cainc yn ei ddyled: heb y llyfr a dalodd amdano, gallai llawer o’r Mabinogi fod ar goll."]),
 P("hywel-fychan", "medieval", "fl. 1380–1410", "Hywel Fychan ap Hywel Goch", "Hywel Fychan ap Hywel Goch", "Scribe of the Red Book of Hergest", "Copïydd Llyfr Coch Hergest", "", "words",
   "Scribe of Buellt who wrote most of the Red Book of Hergest for his master Hopcyn ap Tomas, and so kept the Mabinogion and much of early Welsh literature.",
   "Copïydd o Fuellt a ysgrifennodd y rhan fwyaf o Lyfr Coch Hergest i’w feistr Hopcyn ap Tomas, a thrwy hynny gadw’r Mabinogion a llawer o hen lenyddiaeth Cymru.",
   ["Hywel Fychan ap Hywel Goch came from Buellt, Builth, and was one of the finest Welsh scribes of the Middle Ages. A note in his own hand in another manuscript says that he wrote that book for his master, Hopcyn ap Tomas ab Einion, and from his handwriting scholars know that he was the main scribe of the Red Book of Hergest.",
    "Into the Red Book, between about 1382 and 1410, he copied almost the whole of Welsh literature as it stood: the tales of the Mabinogion, the poetry of the Poets of the Princes, Brut y Brenhinedd and Brut y Tywysogion, the Triads, proverbs and medicine. The Dream of Rhonabwy survives only in his copy. Few people have done more to keep the literature of Wales alive than this patient scribe from Builth."],
   ["Hanai Hywel Fychan ap Hywel Goch o Fuellt, ac roedd yn un o gopïwyr Cymraeg gorau’r Oesoedd Canol. Mae nodyn yn ei law ei hun mewn llawysgrif arall yn dweud iddo ysgrifennu’r llyfr hwnnw i’w feistr, Hopcyn ap Tomas ab Einion, ac o’i lawysgrifen gŵyr ysgolheigion mai ef oedd prif gopïydd Llyfr Coch Hergest.",
    "I’r Llyfr Coch, rhwng tua 1382 a 1410, copïodd bron y cwbl o lenyddiaeth Gymraeg fel yr oedd: chwedlau’r Mabinogion, barddoniaeth Beirdd y Tywysogion, Brut y Brenhinedd a Brut y Tywysogion, y Trioedd, diarhebion a meddygaeth. Yn ei gopi ef yn unig y goroesodd Breuddwyd Rhonabwy. Ychydig a wnaeth fwy i gadw llenyddiaeth Cymru’n fyw na’r copïydd amyneddgar hwn o Lanfair-ym-Muallt."]),
 P("tegid", "industrial", "1792–1852", "John Jones (Tegid)", "John Jones (Tegid)", "Scholar who copied the Red Book for Lady Charlotte Guest", "Ysgolhaig a gopïodd y Llyfr Coch i’r Arglwyddes Charlotte Guest", "John_Jones_(Tegid)", "learning",
   "Welsh scholar and clergyman from Bala, chaplain of Christ Church, Oxford, who copied the tales of the Red Book of Hergest and helped Lady Charlotte Guest translate the Mabinogion.",
   "Ysgolhaig ac offeiriad o’r Bala, caplan Eglwys Crist, Rhydychen, a gopïodd chwedlau Llyfr Coch Hergest ac a helpodd yr Arglwyddes Charlotte Guest i gyfieithu’r Mabinogion.",
   ["John Jones was born at Bala in 1792 and took his bardic name, Tegid, from Llyn Tegid. He went to Jesus College, Oxford, and became a chaplain of Christ Church and a curate in the city, and he was a leading figure among the Welsh scholars of his day: he worked on the spelling of Welsh and edited the poetry of Lewys Glyn Cothi.",
    "In Oxford he had the Red Book of Hergest within reach at Jesus College, and he copied its tales by hand. His copy was the text from which Lady Charlotte Guest made her translation, and he helped her with the hardest passages of the old Welsh. In 1841 he came home to Wales as vicar of Nanhyfer, Nevern, in Pembrokeshire, where he died in 1852. Without Tegid’s patient copying, Lady Charlotte’s Mabinogion could hardly have been made."],
   ["Ganed John Jones yn y Bala yn 1792 a chymerodd ei enw barddol, Tegid, o Lyn Tegid. Aeth i Goleg Iesu, Rhydychen, a dod yn gaplan yn Eglwys Crist ac yn gurad yn y ddinas, ac roedd yn un o ysgolheigion Cymreig blaenaf ei ddydd: gweithiodd ar orgraff y Gymraeg a golygu barddoniaeth Lewys Glyn Cothi.",
    "Yn Rhydychen roedd Llyfr Coch Hergest o fewn cyrraedd yng Ngholeg Iesu, a chopïodd ei chwedlau â llaw. Ei gopi ef oedd y testun y gwnaeth yr Arglwyddes Charlotte Guest ei chyfieithiad ohono, a helpodd hi gyda darnau anoddaf yr hen Gymraeg. Yn 1841 daeth adref i Gymru yn ficer Nanhyfer yn Sir Benfro, lle bu farw yn 1852. Heb gopïo amyneddgar Tegid, prin y gallesid gwneud Mabinogion yr Arglwyddes Charlotte."]),
 P("carnhuanawc", "industrial", "1787–1848", "Thomas Price (Carnhuanawc)", "Thomas Price (Carnhuanawc)", "Historian and patriot who encouraged Lady Charlotte Guest", "Hanesydd a gwladgarwr a galonogodd yr Arglwyddes Charlotte Guest", "Thomas_Price_(Carnhuanawc)", "learning",
   "Vicar of Cwm-du, historian of Wales, champion of the Welsh language and the Welsh harp, and a leader of the Abergavenny Welsh society, who encouraged Lady Charlotte Guest to translate the Mabinogion.",
   "Ficer Cwm-du, hanesydd Cymru, pencampwr yr iaith Gymraeg a’r delyn deires, ac un o arweinwyr Cymreigyddion y Fenni, a galonogodd yr Arglwyddes Charlotte Guest i gyfieithu’r Mabinogion.",
   ["Thomas Price was born in 1787 in the parish of Llanfihangel Bryn Pabuan in Breconshire, and wrote under the bardic name Carnhuanawc. He was vicar of Cwm-du, near Crickhowell, for most of his life, and he gave that life to Wales. He wrote Hanes Cymru, a history of Wales from the earliest times to the death of Llywelyn, published in parts between 1836 and 1842. He championed the Welsh language in the schools and the churches, played and made the Welsh triple harp and trained harpers, and kept close ties with Brittany, helping with the Breton translation of the Bible.",
    "He was one of the leaders of Cymreigyddion y Fenni, the Welsh society of Abergavenny, whose eisteddfodau drew scholars from all over Europe. There he befriended Lady Charlotte Guest and encouraged her in her great work on the Mabinogion. He died in 1848, one of the truest patriots of nineteenth-century Wales."],
   ["Ganed Thomas Price yn 1787 ym mhlwyf Llanfihangel Bryn Pabuan yn sir Frycheiniog, ac ysgrifennai dan yr enw barddol Carnhuanawc. Bu’n ficer Cwm-du, ger Crucywel, am y rhan fwyaf o’i oes, a rhoddodd yr oes honno i Gymru. Ysgrifennodd Hanes Cymru, hanes Cymru o’r amseroedd cynharaf hyd at farw Llywelyn, a gyhoeddwyd fesul rhan rhwng 1836 ac 1842. Bu’n bencampwr dros y Gymraeg yn yr ysgolion a’r eglwysi, canai a gwnâi’r delyn deires a hyfforddai delynorion, a chadwodd gysylltiad agos â Llydaw, gan helpu gyda chyfieithu’r Beibl i’r Llydaweg.",
    "Roedd yn un o arweinwyr Cymreigyddion y Fenni, y gymdeithas Gymraeg yn y Fenni, y denai ei heisteddfodau ysgolheigion o bob rhan o Ewrop. Yno daeth yn gyfaill i’r Arglwyddes Charlotte Guest a’i chalonogi yn ei gwaith mawr ar y Mabinogion. Bu farw yn 1848, un o wladgarwyr cywiraf Cymru’r bedwaredd ganrif ar bymtheg."]),
]

